{"kind":"assert","text":"Design ruling for #11436, the ownership contract for notes-and-decisions publication. DECISION: publication never mints an artifact at a source path. It publishes on its own distinct, stable wire — one reserved publication path per watched path, an exact string derived from the watched path under a reserved prefix that no tracked file path can equal (the implementer confirms the exact spelling against pathCovers, which matches exact strings with no normalisation, so any reserved prefix isolates completely). WHY THIS SHAPE: merge succession owns source paths — planSuccession lands an artifact at every changed path — and after left-live accounting (3c312552) and directional narrowing (7924b1fb), a second live artifact at a source path is a per-push accounting row plus a retirement debt the merger often cannot lawfully pay, as claude revalidation f09d9e55 showed from a receipt left holding twenty-two live rows. A reserved wire removes the collision by construction instead of managing it. THE CONTRACT, point by point. (1) Ownership: the publishing actor authors every artifact on a publication wire and each new publication supersedes that actor own predecessor on the same wire in the same act — self-succession, so retirement authority is never cross-author and abandoned-candidate debt cannot arise from ordinary publication. (2) Exact-head reuse: no diff at a watched path means no act; an identical publication is a replay under its idempotency key, never a duplicate row. Publication does not reuse or touch source-path artifacts. (3) Unsettled candidates: publication neither reads nor retires source-path candidate artifacts; they remain protected by their commitments; a publication artifact rests on its wire predecessor and the observed accepted head, not on source-path artifacts, so no staleness coupling in either direction. (4) Left-live accounting: publication-wire paths never appear in merge receipts because they never equal a changed file path, so ordinary publication adds zero left-live rows and zero merge obligations. (5) Later merge succession: merges keep exclusive ownership of source paths and never touch the wire; after a merge changes watched sources, the next push publishes and supersedes the wire predecessor, so documents resting on publication artifacts flare exactly when the published world moves — the flare-on-move property preserved. (6) Abandonment and retirement: removing a watch line obliges the publisher to bare-supersede its wire terminally; if the publishing actor is retired, a ratifier retires orphaned wires — stated in publish.md as the recovery rule, which also answers the retired-actor half of revalidation finding 5. (7) Post-push proof and per-path isolation preserved: ls-remote verification of the accepted head stands, one wire per watched path stands. BINDING CONDITIONS FOR THE CHILD IMPLEMENTATION REQUEST, from the revalidation, all in scope before any recut merges: publish routes through resolveServerURL exactly like every writing command, refusing an untrustworthy advertisement; publicationDecision must not silently fall back to the local projection on resident-inspect failure and must compare the returned event identifier with the one queried; the outbox permanently-failed wedge (finding 5) gets an explicit exit; the remaining findings 1-4 and 6-8 become test or design conditions verbatim; locking reuses withConfigLock rather than a second flock helper. SEQUENCING: design only under this request; claude recuts from then-current main after receipt f80a1210 lands, and the child implementation request is filed on then-current bases naming this ruling."}